 "seat": {
  "BEST_WHY": "The wall's top surface at (200\u2013430, 400\u2013445) \u2014 the band immediately above the floor line, where the eye actually lands \u2014 is *drawn*. Two courses of blocks with vertical joints running the full width, a horizontal beam at y\u2248425 separating them, and a driven-pin brace at x\u2248300: a vertical timber with two horizontal rungs crossing it. That is a repair someone made, in the frame, at the size the game is played. In frames 1 and 3 the same band is undifferentiated brown cloud \u2014 I measured it: high-frequency detail 2.47 there against 4.19 on the floor two pixels below, versus 2.65/5.34 in frame 2. Second thing: frame 2 is the only frame whose light doesn't break. Sixteen pixels in the entire image have the red channel at 255 (frame 1: 650; frame 3: 15,522), and zero of them are on the character. The consequence is visible on the sprite at (383\u2013432, 458\u2013510): the shield boss at (400\u2013425, 480\u2013505) is mid-tan with a legible ring and dark slot, the sword blade at (388\u2013393, 465\u2013490) has a darker left edge, so the figure holds its silhouette against a bright floor. In 1 and 3 those same pixels are cream.",
  "WORST_WHY": "The props are stamped, not placed. The two barrels at (115\u2013149, 188\u2013216) and (163\u2013197, 188\u2013216) are the same sprite offset 48px \u2014 68.2% pixel-identical, mean absolute difference 1.36. The two crates at (64\u201398, 0\u201326) and (64\u201398, 48\u201374) are the same sprite offset 48px vertically, mean absolute difference 0.15 \u2014 effectively identical. The moss decal at (18\u201342, 55\u201380) reappears unchanged at (10\u201320, 105\u2013118), (30\u201340, 100\u2013115) and (355\u2013380, 145\u2013170). 55% of the frame's 16\u00d716 tiles are exact duplicates of another tile; one tile occurs fifteen times. Second: the pond at (248\u2013368, 0\u2013192) is a rectangle. Flat single teal, a dead-straight vertical edge at x=248, a dead-straight horizontal at y=192, meeting at a 90\u00b0 corner, no bank, no wet mud, no depth value \u2014 water that stops because a tile ran out. Third: both barrels are amputated by the mud/stone tile seam at y=215; their bottom third is simply gone along a straight horizontal line, and their lids are drawn as ~30\u00b0 ellipses while the tile grid is orthogonal top-down, so the props and the ground are in different projections. Fourth: there is no light in it. Not one pixel of the playfield falls below luminance 20 and the corners match the centre \u2014 nothing recedes, everything sits at midtone.",
